<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Habsburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1020 CE – 1415 CE (approx.) · capital Brugg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The modest Swiss county around Habsburg Castle in the Aargau, the ancestral home of the dynasty that came to rule Austria, Spain and much of Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1020 to 1030 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radbot-of-klettgau">Radbot</a>, a count in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/landgraviate-of-klettgau">Klettgau</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werner-i-of-strasbourg">Werner of Strasbourg</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-castle">Habichtsburg</a> ('hawk's castle') on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brugg">Brugg</a>. Radbot's descendants took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> and built up lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and around Zurich, founding the family monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muri-abbey">Muri</a> in 1027.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts combined allodial land, advocacies over monasteries and comital rights in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgau">Thurgau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zurichgau">Zürichgau</a>. They inherited much of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-kyburg">County of Kyburg</a> in 1264. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf IV</a> was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1273 as Rudolf I and in 1278 defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a>, gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1282 the family centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and the Swiss lands slipped away to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Swiss Confederacy</a>, notably after defeats at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-morgarten">Morgarten</a> in 1315 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Sempach</a> in 1386. In 1415, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iv-duke-of-austria">Duke Frederick IV</a> was outlawed, Bern conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-castle">Habsburg Castle</a> itself.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1020 CE – 1415 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.00%;width:7.86%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1020 CE – 1415 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Brugg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Radbot, Albert IV of Habsburg, Rudolf I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Habsburg, Habsburg Aargau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1218&amp;focus=county-of-habsburg" aria-label="County of Ha